  "body": {
    "id": "lem:exact-regret-path",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "statement": "For every \\(c>0\\) and \\(t=c/(1+c)\\), the causal penalized-welfare regret satisfies \\((1+c)\\sqrt n\\,\\mathscr R_n(c)=G(u_n,E_n)(t)=T_n(t)\\). Thus the interior process is an identified causal regret curve, and its values at \\(t=0\\) and \\(t=1\\) are defined by the two compactified limits.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "c",
      "t",
      "n",
      "Rreg",
      "G",
      "un",
      "En",
      "Tn"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:penalized-welfare",
      "def:oracle-learned-lotteries",
      "def:causal-regret",
      "def:compactified-path",
      "def:oracle-regret-path",
      "lem:bregman-identity",
      "ass:consistency",
      "ass:randomization",
      "lem:hard-endpoint",
      "lem:high-endpoint"
    ],
    "justification": "This identity prevents the Gaussian path from becoming a detached statistical surrogate and pins every interior coordinate to the planner's causal objective.",
    "gap": "The presolve algebra and Fang--Ridder--Xie fixed-weight criterion motivate the identity, but the full local normalization and endpoint compactification are not a published result.",
    "consumer": "The simultaneous band can be interpreted directly in penalized-welfare units at every interior temperature selected measurably from the observed sample or the plug-in frontier.",
    "proof_tex": "Fix \\(c>0\\).  In stratum \\(j\\), the term involving the reference mean\n\\(\\mu_{n,j0}\\) is constant over lotteries.  Since\n\\(u_{n,j}=\\sqrt n\\,\\Delta_{n,j}\\) and\n\\(\\lambda_n(c)=c/\\sqrt n\\), multiplication of the nonconstant part of\nthe welfare criterion by \\(\\sqrt n\\) gives\n\\[\n (Ru_{n,j})^\\top\\pi_j-c\\,\\mathrm{KL}(\\pi_j\\|b_j).                      \\tag{13}\n\\]\nThe Gibbs variational identity, obtained by substituting the definition of\n\\(q_{c,b}\\), is\n\\[\n \\sup_{\\pi\\in\\Delta^{K-1}}\n \\{v^\\top\\pi-c\\,\\mathrm{KL}(\\pi\\|b)\\}\n =\\Psi_{c,b}(v),\n \\quad\\text{with unique maximizer }q_{c,b}(v).                         \\tag{14}\n\\]\nFor \\(w=v+z\\), the same substitution gives\n\\[\n c\\,\\mathrm{KL}\\{q_{c,b}(w)\\|b\\}\n =w^\\top q_{c,b}(w)-\\Psi_{c,b}(w).                                    \\tag{15}\n\\]\nTake \\(v=Ru_{n,j}\\) and \\(z=RE_{n,j}\\).  The definitions of the oracle and\nlearned lotteries and (14)--(15) show, stratum by stratum, that the oracle\nvalue of (13) minus its value at the learned lottery is\n\\[\n \\Psi_{c,b_j}(v)-\\Psi_{c,b_j}(v+z)+z^\\top q_{c,b_j}(v+z)\n =\\ell_{c,b_j}(v,z).                                                   \\tag{16}\n\\]\nSumming (16) with weights \\(p_j\\) proves\n\\[\n \\sqrt n\\,\\mathscr R_n(c)\n =\\sum_{j\\in\\mathcal J}p_j\\ell_{c,b_j}(Ru_{n,j},RE_{n,j}).\n\\]\nMultiplication by \\(1+c\\), the definition \\(t=c/(1+c)\\), and\n\\(\\mathrm{def:compactified\\mbox{-}path}\\) give\n\\[\n (1+c)\\sqrt n\\,\\mathscr R_n(c)=G(u_n,E_n)(t)=T_n(t).\n\\]\nConsistency and randomization identify every \\(\\mu_{n,ja}\\), and hence\nthe causal welfare functional used on the left; equation (16), rather than\na definition, proves its equality to the statistical path.  The values at\n\\(t=0\\) and \\(t=1\\) are the limits proved in\n\\(\\mathrm{lem:hard\\mbox{-}endpoint}\\) and\n\\(\\mathrm{lem:high\\mbox{-}endpoint}\\).",
